{\rtf1\mac\deff2 {\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss Chicago;}{\f2\froman New York;}{\f3\fswiss Geneva;}{\f4\fmodern Monaco;}{\f13\fnil Zapf Dingbats;}{\f15\fnil N Helvetica Narrow;}{\f16\fnil Palatino;}{\f20\froman Times;}{\f21\fswiss Helvetica;}{\f22\fmodern Courier;}{\f23\ftech Symbol;}{\f256\fnil VT320;}{\f1085\fnil BAeSEMAfont;}}{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;}{\stylesheet{\s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 \sbasedon222\snext254 heading 2;}{\s255\qc\sa240\sl480\keepn \b\f20\fs48 \sbasedon222\snext255 heading 1;}{\qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext0 Normal;}{\s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext1 BL;}{\s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 \sbasedon222\snext2 HD;}{\s3\qj\fi-360\li720\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext3 ZA;}{\s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 \sbasedon222\snext4 CD;}{\s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext5 PM;}{\s6\fi-360\li1800\sa240\sl240\tx1800 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext6 VL;}{\s7\qj\li360\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 \sbasedon222\snext7 ZB;}}\paperw11906\paperh16838\margt1800\margb1800\widowctrl \sectd \linemod0\cols1 {\footer \pard\plain \qc \f16 \chpgn \par }\pard\plain \s2\qc\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 {\fs36 OPL DIALOG ROUTINES FOR PSION S3A}\par \pard \s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 Introduction\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This code was originally written as part of my "Address Book" shareware application. I found that the Dialog facilities provided by OPL were too limited for what I wanted to do. In particular I wanted the ability to: \par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab perform dialogs asynchronously (i.e. handle key presses and other events outside the dialogs),;\par \'a5\tab to have more than eight dialog entries;\par \'a5\tab to have multi-line entries.\par \pard\plain \s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 Structure\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The file DIALG1V3.OPL contains 'interface' procedures, which are called from user's code. They all have the prefix DG, and their use is described below.\par The file DIALG2V3.OPL contains other procedures which support the interface procedures.\par In most cases the interface procedures return pointers to data structures, which are commonly used as parameters in subsequent procedure calls. The procedures usually return a 'null' pointer (i.e. 0) if an error prevents the function from being completed. The most likely error is insufficient memory.\par A typical sequence for using the routines is:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab Define the dialog;\par \'a5\tab Display the dialog\par \'a5\tab Input key presses;\par \'a5\tab Call dialog routines to handle key presses;\par \'a5\tab Read back values entered in dialog fields;\par \'a5\tab Clear the dialog.\par \pard\plain \s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 Using the Routines\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Defining a Dialog\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 First you must initialise a dialog with the following call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 dg%=DGinit%:(title$)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {title}{\plain $\tab }is the title for the dialog;\par {\f22 dg%}{\plain \tab }is the dialog handle (used in subsequent calls).\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This is very similar to the OPL DGINIT function.\par Next you define each field in the dialog. Currently only text fields are supported, which are added to the dialog by these calls:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGtext%:(dg%,label$,flags%)\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\f22 dg%}{\plain \tab }is the dialog handle;\par {\plain label%\tab }is the label for the field;\par {\plain flags%\tab }is a bit mask defining the characteristics of the field, formed by ORing together the following values:\par \pard\plain \s6\fi-360\li1800\sa240\sl240\tx1800 \f16 1\tab the field is 'selectable';\par 2\tab the field is modifiable;\par 4\tab the field is 'replaceable' (this is explained later under 'default text');\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%\tab }is the field handle (used in subsequent calls).\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This is very similar in function to DEDIT and DTEXT, except that no initial contents are defined at this stage. Use this call to specify the initial contents:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 ret%=DGaddtx%:(field%,text$,def%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%\tab }is the field handle;\par {\plain text$\tab }is the text string;\par {\plain def%\tab }is a flag indicating this is 'default' text (see later, set to 0 for 'simple' operation);\par {\plain ret%\tab }is 0 for success, otherwise a negative error code.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can call this repeatedly for the same field, to append further text. If a string is terminated by a CHR$(21) then subsequent text will always be displayed starting on a fresh line.\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Displaying a Dialog\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Once you have defined your dialog you can display it with the following call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGshow%:(dg%,font%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%\tab }is the dialog handle;\par font%\tab is the font to be used (specified as for gFONT);\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the first selectable field (or -1 if there are no selectable fields).\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This is similar to DIALOG {\ul except} that control is returned immediately to the calling program (rather than waiting for the dialog to be completed). You can DGshow% any number of times at any point in a dialog's existence; e.g. the dialog can be zoomed by supplying different values for font%.\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Handling Key Presses\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Input from the keyboard is handled by the calling program, e.g. by calling KEY, GETEVENT, etc. The program must then call an appropriate dialog routine to handle the key press.\par For 'movement' keys (e.g. up, down, home, page up) call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGmove%:(dg%,key%,mod%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%\tab }is the dialog handle;\par key%\tab is the key code;\par mod%\tab is the modifier (i.e. as returned by KMOD);\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the new 'current' field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 To insert a character at the current position call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGinsrt%:(dg%,text$)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%\tab }is the dialog handle;\par text$\tab is the character to be inserted;\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the 'current' field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If CHR$(21) is supplied as text$ then a new line is opened up in the dialog.\par To perform a delete operation call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGdelet%:(dg%,mod%,paste%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%\tab }is the dialog handle;\par mod%\tab is the modifier (i.e. as returned by KMOD);\par {\plain paste%}\tab is a pointer to a paste buffer;\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the 'current' field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If any text is highlighted then this is removed, and if paste% is not zero copied to the designated paste buffer (see 'Cutting and Pasting' later). Otherwise the character to the left of the current position is removed (or to the right if mod% indicates shift down, or characters to the end of line if mod% indicates psion-shift down).\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Reading Back Values\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 When a dialog has been completed it is necessary to read back the values that have been entered in each field. First find out how many lines of text have been entered in a field by calling:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 lines%=DGflns%:(field%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain lines%}\tab is the number of lines.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Then call the following routine to read back the first line:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 ptext%=DGfstln%:(field%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain ptext%}\tab is a pointer to the text in the first line.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The actual text string can be obtained by calling:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 PEEK$(ptext%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Then call the following repeatedly to read back subsequent lines:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 ptext%=DGnxtln%:(field%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain ptext%}\tab is a pointer to the text in the next line.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The following routine indicates if the field has been modified (by user input):\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 mod%=DGmodfd%:(field%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain mod%}\tab is non-zero if the field has been modified\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The following routine indicates if any field in the dialog has been modified (by user input):\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 mod%=DGmoddg%:(dg%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain mod%}\tab is non-zero if any field has been modified.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can use the following call to find out the currently selected field:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGselct%:(dg%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the 'current' field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 \par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Clearing a Dialog\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Once a dialog has been completed, call the following to clear the dialog from the screen, and free all associated memory allocations:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 dg%=DGclear%:(dg%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle (always returned as zero);\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Cutting and Pasting\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Call the following routine to copy the highlighted text into a paste buffer:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 lines%=DGcopy%:(dg%,paste%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain paste%}\tab is the address of a paste buffer (an array of 3 integers);\par {\plain lines%\tab }is the number of lines of text copied (0 if no highlighted text to copy, -1 if the copy can't be completed, due to lack of memory etc.).\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This text can be pasted back into the dialog at the current position with the following call:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 field%=DGpaste%:(dg%,paste%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain paste%}\tab is the address of the paste buffer;\par {\plain field%\tab }is the handle of the 'current' field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 A paste buffer which has been set up by a call to DGdelet% can be used in the same way.\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Buttons\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can add buttons to a dialog by calling the following procedure during definition of the dialog, to define each button required:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 button%=DGbuttn%:(dg%,key%,text$)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain key%}\tab is the key code to activate the button;\par {\plain text$}\tab is the text to display above the button;\par {\plain button%\tab }is the button handle.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can use the following procedure to check if a key press matches the code specified for any button in the dialog:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 button%=DGpshbt%:(dg%,key%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain key%}\tab is the entered key code;\par {\plain button%\tab }is the handle of the matching button, or 0 if there is no matching button.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The selected button is shown 'depressed'. It can be returned to the 'released' state by calling:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 button%=DGrelbt%:(dg%,key%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain dg%}\tab is the dialog handle;\par {\plain key%}\tab is the entered key code;\par {\plain button%\tab }is the handle of the matching button, or 0 if there is no matching button.\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Default Text\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 An advanced feature is the ability to associate 'default' text with a field. This text is displayed in grey and cannot be modified, but it can be replaced with fresh text input by the user. If a field with default text is completely cleared by a call to DGdelet%, then the default text is displayed.\par If a field has default text which is to be shown initially:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab Call {\plain DGtext% }with {\plain flags%=5 }(replaceable but not modifiable);\par \'a5\tab Call {\plain DGaddtx%: }for the default text with {\plain def%=-1};\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 If a field has default text, and also some different initial text:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab Call {\plain DGtext% }with {\plain flags%=7 }(replaceable and modifiable);\par \'a5\tab Call {\plain DGaddtx%: }for the initial text with {\plain def%=}0;\par \'a5\tab Call {\plain DGaddtx%: }for the default text with {\plain def%=-1};\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 On completion of a dialog you can find out if a field contains the default text, or text which has been entered by the user, by calling:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 mod%=DGismod%:(field%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain mod%}\tab is non-zero if the field contains modifiable text (i.e. NOT the default text).\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Other Functions\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can find out the current cursor position and bounds of highlighted text within a modifiable field by calling:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 DGgetxy:(field%,pxy%)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain pxy%\tab }is a pointer to an array of 4 integers.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 After the call the array contains the following values (in order):\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab X position of cursor;\par \'a5\tab Y position of cursor;\par \'a5\tab X position of other bound of highlighted text (-1 if nothing highlighted);\par \'a5\tab Y position of other bound of highlighted text.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You can use the following procedure to modify the text in a field during dialog entry:\par \pard\plain \s4\li360\sa240\sl200\keep\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f22\fs20 ret%=DGmodtx%:(field%,text$)\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 where:\par \pard\plain \s5\fi-1080\li1440\sa240\sl240\tx1440 \f16 {\plain field%}\tab is the field handle;\par {\plain text$}\tab is the new text;\par {\plain ret%}\tab is non-zero if the call is successful.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 Currently this can only be used on non-modifiable fields containing a single line of text.\par \pard\plain \s254\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21 Example\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The file DIALOGEX.OPL contains a simple example program using some of the procedures. Note that no error handling is included; a real program should check the return values from each call. Also it is preferable to use GETEVENT to read key presses rather than KEY; this then enables the program to respond to system events as well.\par \pard\plain \s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 Future Developments\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This initial version was implemented to meet the requirements of my 'Address Book' program. However it has been structured so that it should be easy to add extra functionality. In particular:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab Support for other field types (numeric, choice, date, filenames, etc.);\par \'a5\tab Support for more flexible dynamic changing of field characteristics and contents, e.g. making fields selectable/modifiable in response to user input in another other field.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 The code as written is only suitable for the Psion Series 3A. However I believe it can be adapted for other platforms, e.g. Series 3. The changes required include:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab Replacing UADD/USUB with +/-;\par \'a5\tab Replacing gXBORDER with gBORDER;\par \'a5\tab Eliminating use of grey plane.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 I am currently in the process of replacing all the PEEK and POKE statements with OPP Macros. This should make the code more readable and intelligible!\par \pard\plain \s2\sb40\sa280\sl280\keepn\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \b\f21\fs28 Distribution and Use\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 This code may be freely distributed provided that:\par \pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-360\li360\sa240\sl240\tx360 \f16 \'a5\tab No charge is made, other than to cover reasonable costs;\par \'a5\tab These notes are distributed with  it.\par \pard\plain \qj\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760 \f16 You are free to use this code for personal purposes. However if you find it useful I would ask you to make a donation to a charity I am closely involved with. Reading Steiner School Project (Registered Charity ???) provides education in the Reading area based on the principles of the Austrian philosopher and educationalist Dr Rudolf Steiner. Please send cheques (payable to RSSP) to:\par \pard\plain \s7\li360\sa240\sl240\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx